[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Schema Definition Language (SDL) in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Schema Definition Language (SDL) is the core syntax used in GraphQL to define the shape of your data, the types available, and how clients can query or mutate them. It serves as a contract between the client and the server, ensuring that both parties agree on the exact data structure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use SDL?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Language Agnostic: SDL is independent of the programming language used to implement the backend."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Human Readable: It provides a clear, self-documenting blueprint of your API domain."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tooling Support: Enables powerful ecosystem tools like code generators, IDE autocompletion, and schema validation."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Object Types"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object types are the building blocks of any GraphQL schema. They represent a kind of object you can fetch from your service, and fields represent the properties of that object."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"type User {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  email: String
  age: Int
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Scalars and Modifiers"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL comes with default scalar types: Int, Float, String, Boolean, and ID. You can also use modifiers to dictate nullability and lists."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The exclamation mark (!) means the field is non-nullable and will never return null."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Square brackets ([ ]) denote a list type, such as [String] or [User!]."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Root Operation Types"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every schema requires a Query type, and optionally can include Mutation and Subscription types. These define the entry points for your API operations."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"type Query {
  getUser(id: ID!): User
  allUsers: [User!]!
}

type Mutation {
  createUser(name: String!, email: String!): User!
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering SDL is the first step toward building robust GraphQL APIs. By clearly defining types, queries, and mutations, you establish a reliable and scalable foundation for your application architecture."}]
